            <div xml:id="d553">

                <pb facs="0776" n="762" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg762"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.93/1750: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-21T12:00:00+07:00">March 21,
                            1944—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received March 21—7:55 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>496. Admiral Yang, Chief of Chinese Intelligence who has been a good friend and
                    reliable informant, stated to Counselor on March 20 that no really reliable
                    reports had been received regarding the incident referred to in our 479, March
                    17, and previous, but that the planes had apparently come into Sinkiang from
                    direction of Kobdo, Outer Mongolia.</p>
                <p>The place name given as Chingho appears on one map to be Chenghwa about 120 miles
                    west of Kobdo.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
